# Test PDF corpus and directory (large binary collection)
#
# `test_PDFs/*`, NOT `test_PDFs/`: git does not descend into an EXCLUDED
# DIRECTORY, so a `!` re-include under `test_PDFs/` is unreachable and
# `git add` refuses the path. Excluding the CONTENTS keeps the directory
# traversable so the single re-include below can take effect. (Verified: with
# the trailing-slash form, `git add test_PDFs/crazyones-pdfa.pdf` failed with
# "The following paths are ignored".)
test_PDFs/*
# meta-audit follow-up F45 (phase-46): ONE 16 KB file is re-included. Three
# tests hard-assert `fs::exists(test_PDFs/crazyones-pdfa.pdf)` BEFORE they
# consult the conformance checker, so on any clean checkout — including every
# CI run, since actions/checkout runs `git clean -ffdx` — they failed on the
# missing input, not on anything about PDF/A. The "large binary collection"
# rationale above does not apply to a single 16 KB document, and the
# alternative (converting the asserts to GTEST_SKIP) would delete the
# gold-standard reference check rather than fix it.
#
# NOTE FOR ANYONE EDITING THIS BLOCK: `git check-ignore` is the WRONG probe for
# a `!` re-include — it exits 0 on the re-include match and reads as "ignored".
# Verify with `git ls-files --others --ignored --exclude-standard`, which lists
# what is ACTUALLY still ignored. (Phase-44 learned this the hard way.)
!test_PDFs/crazyones-pdfa.pdf
# GitHub #189 (LOCAL half, pipeline p87 phase-4): a SECOND 77 KB file is
# re-included, for the same reason and on the same terms as the one above.
#
# `test_PDFs/multicolumn.pdf` is the ONLY other corpus file any test resolves
# BY NAME -- test_layout_extraction_corpus.cpp:332 and
# test_fidelity_r3_07_text_extraction.cpp:169 both GTEST_SKIP without it, and
# one of them is the project's only column-major reading-order oracle. On a
# clean checkout that oracle did not run, and the run still read green.
#
# It is a synthetic LaTeX Lorem-Ipsum two-column document (3 pages, pdfTeX
# 1.40.21, author field literally "Your Name"): no third-party content, no
# personal data, no licence encumbrance. Scanned clean for the build account
# name across both the raw bytes and all 11 inflated FlateDecode streams --
# the project's text-only detector cannot read a .pdf, so
# tools/gate/scan_binary_path_hygiene.py does that job and is what was run.
#
# THE OTHER 69 FILES ARE DELIBERATELY STILL IGNORED, and not for size alone:
# none has documented provenance, and several plainly should never be
# published from this repository -- a real business invoice bearing the
# owner's own domain, a commercially-published book, a 279 MB set of
# construction specifications for a named school, and vendor datasheets from
# HPE, PrinceXML and Smallpdf. Tracking those to make a corpus-glob test stop
# skipping would trade a test-coverage gap for a licensing and privacy one.
!test_PDFs/multicolumn.pdf
/test_PDFs.7z

