# Phase 6: Rust build artifacts
/rust/tantivy_bridge/target/
# NOTE: Cargo.lock is intentionally COMMITTED (not ignored). tantivy_bridge
# is an application component built as part of PDFE, so the lock must be
# pinned for reproducible builds. CI does `git clean -ffdx` on checkout,
# which deleted the (formerly gitignored) lock and forced cargo to
# re-resolve all 164 transitive crates to latest — pulling a `time` crate
# combination with a conflicting trait impl (error[E0119] in
# tantivy-common-0.11.0 → time) that does not compile on rustc 1.95,
# breaking every validation/sanitizer build (run 27436954949, 2026-06-12).
# Committing the lock pins the known-good versions (time 0.3.47).

# --- Security test harness (opt-in, gitignore generated artifacts) ---
# Generated PDF payloads — generators are committed; PDFs are
# regenerated on demand via scripts/security/gen_malicious_corpus.ps1.
# Re-includes for the manifest + provenance docs that DO get committed.
tests/security/.venv/
# Phase 2 AC requires PDF-specific globs (not a blanket ** ignore) so
# the manifest, README, SOURCE.txt, and any future tracked metadata
# files survive a fresh checkout. Categories live in subdirs (goto/,
# js/, launch/, etc) so /**/* is needed.
tests/security/corpus/malicious_pdf/**/*.pdf
tests/security/corpus/malicious_pdf/**/*.svg
tests/security/corpus/malicious_pdf/**/*.xml
# Re-includes for committed provenance.
!tests/security/corpus/malicious_pdf/SOURCE.txt
!tests/security/corpus/manifest.json
!tests/security/corpus/README.md
!tests/security/corpus/.gitkeep
!tests/security/corpus/malicious_pdf/.gitkeep
# Transient run reports — only the canonical baselines under
# regression_baselines/ are committed. NOTE: glob form (trailing /*)
# rather than directory form (trailing /) — `!` re-includes below
# can only override file globs, not directory ignores.
# Per-developer A/V exclusion helper (parameterized by user's repo
# path; not part of the suite). The reusable scaffold for adding
# the exclusions is documented in scripts/security/verify_av_exclusions.ps1
# (which prints the exact Add-MpPreference command on FAIL).
tests/security/Defender_Exclusions.ps1
# Phase 4-8 external tools downloaded by install_tools.ps1
# (gitleaks, trufflehog, osv-scanner, etc.) -- these are large
# binaries that should be installed locally, not committed.
# NOTE: glob form (`tools/*`) rather than directory form (`tools/`). A
# DIRECTORY ignore stops git descending at all, so a later `!` can never
# re-include anything beneath it — the same trap this file already documents
# for tests/security/reports. The glob form ignores each immediate child
# individually, which the `!` lines below can then override. (Before F14 this
# was `tools/`; tools/gate and tools/vcpkg-overlays only appeared to be
# re-included because their files were already tracked, and tracked files are
# never affected by .gitignore.)
tools/*
# but KEEP the committed gate tooling (check scripts, parity suite, baselines) — force-add pattern made durable
!tools/gate/
!tools/gate/**

# --- re-ignores for tools/gate/ ------------------------------------------
# The `!tools/gate/**` negation above voids every blanket rule for this
# directory (last match wins), so each one has to be restated here. Without
# this, a credentials file or a build cache dropped in this directory is
# COMMITTABLE despite the rules at the top of this file saying otherwise.
tools/gate/__pycache__/
tools/gate/.env
tools/gate/*.env
tools/gate/__pycache__/
# meta-audit follow-up F44 (backlog rows F38-b / F46): KEEP the runner-operations
# scripts — tools/ops/runner_watchdog.ps1 (the heartbeat watchdog that replaces
# the untracked one beside the runner) and tools/ops/register_runner_service.ps1
# (the autostart helper). Both are named by tools/gate/test_runner_watchdog_heartbeat.py,
# which runs in fast-checks on a fresh checkout: without this re-include the
# blanket `tools/*` above would have left them untracked and that lane would have
# gone red on files that exist only on the author's disk. The .ps1 SCRIPTS are
# committed; nothing they produce is (heartbeats and logs live beside the runner,
# outside this repo).
!tools/ops/
!tools/ops/**

# --- re-ignores for tools/ops/ ------------------------------------------
# The `!tools/ops/**` negation above voids every blanket rule for this
# directory (last match wins), so each one has to be restated here. Without
# this, a credentials file or a build cache dropped in this directory is
# COMMITTABLE despite the rules at the top of this file saying otherwise.
tools/ops/__pycache__/
tools/ops/.env
tools/ops/*.env
# Runner deployment package (phase-24 prerequisite): the scripts + the
# requirements manifest that make standing up an additional pdfe-windows
# runner repeatable. Same reasoning as tools/ops above -- the blanket
# `tools/*` would otherwise leave these untracked, and a deployment package
# that exists only on one machine is not a deployment package.
!tools/runner-deployment/
!tools/runner-deployment/**

# vcpkg triplet overlays (GitHub #177). CMakePresets.json REFERENCES
# tools/vcpkg-triplets as VCPKG_OVERLAY_TRIPLETS for all five Windows presets, so
# without this re-include the blanket `tools/*` above would leave the triplet
# untracked and every configure would fail on a fresh checkout looking for a file
# that exists only on the author's disk. Caught before the commit, by checking
# `git check-ignore` rather than trusting that `git add` had worked: adding an
# ignored file exits 0 and stages NOTHING.
!tools/vcpkg-triplets/
!tools/vcpkg-triplets/**

# --- re-ignores for tools/vcpkg-triplets/ --------------------------------
# The negation above voids every blanket rule for this directory (last match
# wins), so restate them, exactly as the tools/gate and tools/ops blocks do.
tools/vcpkg-triplets/__pycache__/
tools/vcpkg-triplets/.env
tools/vcpkg-triplets/*.env

# --- re-ignores for tools/runner-deployment/ ------------------------------------------
# The `!tools/runner-deployment/**` negation above voids every blanket rule for this
# directory (last match wins), so each one has to be restated here. Without
# this, a credentials file or a build cache dropped in this directory is
# COMMITTABLE despite the rules at the top of this file saying otherwise.
tools/runner-deployment/__pycache__/
tools/runner-deployment/.env
tools/runner-deployment/*.env
!tools/runner-deployment/.env.example
# ...but NOT a .env there. The blanket `.env` rule near the top of this file
# does not survive the negation above: last match wins, so `!tools/runner-
# deployment/**` re-includes it and a credentials file dropped beside the script
# that reads it becomes COMMITTABLE. Verified empirically -- a .env created
# there showed up as `??` in git status while a root-level one did not.
#
# This is the worst possible place for that hole: next to Set-R2Config.ps1 is
# exactly where an operator would naturally put it.
tools/runner-deployment/.env
tools/runner-deployment/*.env
# Same hole, third instance. `__pycache__/` at the top of this file is defeated
# by the negation above exactly as `.env` was -- and line 309 shows someone
# already had to write `tools/gate/__pycache__/` for the same reason, which is
# the tell that this is a CLASS and not three incidents. Any blanket rule is
# void inside a `!dir/**` negation; each such directory needs its own
# re-ignores.
tools/runner-deployment/__pycache__/
# The tracked TEMPLATE must stay visible -- it holds no secrets and exists so
# nobody has to guess the key names.
!tools/runner-deployment/.env.example
# KEEP committed vcpkg overlay ports (hermetic dependency-hash overrides for CI)
!tools/vcpkg-overlays/
!tools/vcpkg-overlays/**

