# Meta-audit follow-up F39 (backlog row D-6). The two-stage clang-cl libFuzzer
# build described by .github/workflows/windows-fuzz.yml produces two large
# untracked trees at the repo root, and `build/` alone does not cover them:
#   build-fuzz-deps*   stage 1, the x64-windows-static vcpkg dependency install
#   build-fuzz*        stage 2, the instrumented build tree (the fuzz_image_decode
#                      binary alone is ~100 MB, its PDB ~230 MB)
# The workflow uses build-fuzz-deps/ and build-fuzz/; F39 used
# build-fuzz-deps-static/ and build-fuzz-local/ (the latter matching the existing
# build_fuzz_local.bat helper). The glob covers all four so a `git add -A` after
# a local fuzz build cannot sweep multiple GB into a commit.
build-fuzz*/

# Install-PdfeRunner.ps1 writes a per-machine run log beside itself. It records
# what THIS host tried to install, so it is machine-local state, not source.
tools/runner-deployment/install-log.txt

# --- public-repo hygiene (quota-and-scrub phase-4) --------------------------
# scratchpad/ was NEVER ignored -- only *.dmp inside it was. It has held crash
# dumps and credential-shaped test strings during development, and it is
# untracked only by discipline. That is not a control worth relying on in a
# repository that is going public, where one `git add -A` publishes it.
scratchpad/
prompt.txt

# Personal build wrappers. configure.bat was tracked while every one of its
# siblings (build_repro.bat, build_fuzz_local*.bat, build_preflight.bat) was
# not, and it hardcodes an operator's absolute paths. No doc, workflow or script
# references it. Untracked, consistent with the siblings.
configure.bat
build_repro.bat
build_fuzz_local.bat
build_fuzz_local_inc.bat
build_preflight.bat

# SEC-05 / GitHub #37 -- the campaign-time staging area for the malicious
# payload subset. TIGHTENING, not relaxing: those payloads phone home, trip
# contributor antivirus on clone, and are already excluded at their source
# (tests/security/corpus/malicious_pdf/). Without this rule a staged campaign
# could commit them by accident, which is the whole thing that policy prevents.
tests/security/fuzz/seeds/_malicious_overlay/

# GitHub #160 -- raw per-shard gtest output from tests/scripts/run_gtest.py.
# TIGHTENING, not relaxing. A raw gtest XML carries the build account name once
# per test via its absolute file= paths (roughly 380 occurrences per shard), and
# this repo has already leaked that name twice by committing such a file. The
# COMMITTED evidence is always the sanitized merge at
# docs/audit/**/gtest-results.xml, produced by tests/scripts/sanitize_gtest_xml.py
# -- never these shards, which exist only between the run and the merge.
#
# Deliberately scoped to the raw-output directory name so it cannot swallow the
# sanitized artifact: `git add` on an ignored path exits 0 and stages NOTHING,
# so an over-broad rule here would silently drop real evidence.
#
# GitHub #223 -- the rule this comment describes was never actually written.
# Until now the only rule beneath it was `docs/audit/**/rerun/`, which does NOT
# match the run-output directories `--output-dir` actually names, and which
# matches nothing in the tree today (no `docs/audit/**/rerun/` directory exists
# as of 2026-09-18 -- checked, not assumed). It is kept below rather than
# removed: it is harmless, and deleting a rule whose original purpose is not
# recorded is how an exclusion gets lost. The raw shards were protected only
# INCIDENTALLY, by the blanket `*.xml` and `*.log` rules far higher up: any
# future non-xml/non-log artifact run_gtest.py emitted would have been
# unprotected while this comment claimed otherwise. The directory rule is the
# designed protection; the blanket rules are now belt-and-braces, not the belt.
#
# Scope note (the constraint above, restated because it is what makes this rule
# safe): both patterns end in `/` and name a DIRECTORY whose basename cannot
# occur on the sanitized-evidence path, so neither can match
# docs/audit/**/gtest-results.xml.
tests/_gtest_*/
docs/audit/**/rerun/
